    "question": {
      ":question/id": "23968e86-3a76-5fe3-b2bc-5ec6853ae392",
      ":question/problem": "What is the antiderivative of $\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}}?$",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Note that $\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}}={x}^{-4}.$ So, we can use the power rule with $n=-4$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {x}^{-4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{-4+1}}{-4+1}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{{x}^{-3}}{3}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3{x}^{3}}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"
    },
