      ":question/problem": "Given that $f(x)={x}^{-1/7}$ and that ${F}^{'}(x)=f(x)$, calculate $F(x).$",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Remember that integration is the opposite of differentiation. So, if ${F}^{'}(x)=f(x),$ then integrating both sides of this equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{'}(x) & =f(x) \\\\ \\int {F}^{'}(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ F(x) & =\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can use the power rule with $n=-\\frac{1}{7}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(x) & =\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int {x}^{-1/7}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{-1/7+1}}{(-\\frac{1}{7}+1)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{6/7}}{(\\frac{6}{7})}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{6}{x}^{6/7}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"
    },
